00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.560
Let's start by describing
the rise of microservices.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.520
There are several architectural
styles in software.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.850
Monoliths, for instance.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.600
Perhaps, your organization
has monoliths.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.690
We're going to take you on a
journey today from Monoliths

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:45.480
to microservices to
event-driven microservices.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.000
Let's start with monoliths.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.793
They're fairly
easy to understand.

00:01:48.793 --> 00:01:50.210
As you can see on
the screen here,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.435
here's an e-commerce store
with six component services.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.540
There's a few problems that
are inherent in any monolith.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.100
Scalability.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.660
Component services scale as
a group and not individually.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.039
Extensibility.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.930
It's hard to add
or update services.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.100
And lastly, coupling.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.832
There are often coupled
dependencies between services

00:02:17.832 --> 00:02:19.540
that you have to deal
with in a monolith.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.280
So to address some
of these problems,

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.870
you might want to chart a
migration to microservices

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.210
by pulling out
component services

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.185
into separate services.

00:02:35.328 --> 00:02:37.120
Let's take a look at
the example over here.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.570
We've pulled out a couple
of component services--

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:40.990
the newsletter and the shop.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.860
As we continue this migration
and pull out a third service,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.710
you start to see the connections
between the services develop.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.710
As we pull out a fourth service,
the connections between them

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:56.490
deepen further.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.360
We've now completed the
migration to microservices.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.800
While we've started to
address some of the issues

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.530
with scalability,
extensibility, and coupling,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.930
we haven't done so completely.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.520
We've improved on
scalability, but there's still

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.120
the question of whether the
communication between services

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.220
is synchronous or asynchronous.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.110
That makes a difference.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.590
We've improved on extensibility.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.100
However, the new
services that we add

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.980
may still require
that we make updates

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.890
to existing services that
are going to communicate

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.500
with that new service.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.510
And lastly, while we've
made coupling looser,

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.880
it's possible that
different services may still

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.130
have couple dependencies--

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.320
on a message cue,
for instance, which

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.080
you may want to take a
dependency on if you're

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.555
looking to protect against
intermittent failure

00:03:51.555 --> 00:03:52.055
of services.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.500
In addition to
the three problems

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.670
that we've only
solved partially,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.380
microservices also introduce
three new problems.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.040
For one, there's a spider web
of point-to-point integrations

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.630
like you saw in
the previous slide.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.290
There is the potential for
expensive inter-service

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.470
communication, especially if the
communication between services

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.560
is pull-style,
saved via polling.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.740
There's also the potential for
a non-uniform communication

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.315
format between services.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.590
We continue our evolution onto
event-driven architectures.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:40.130
But first, let's start by
describing, what is an event?

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.200
There's three concepts
to think about here--

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.500
occurrences,
events, and actions.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.690
In the operation of
a software system,

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.720
an occurrence is when something
happens, a statement of fact

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.510
about something happening.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.030
An event is a record
expressing that occurrence

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.690
and its context.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:00.930
And an action will
take place based

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.800
on an occurrence, which is
communicated to a service

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.210
through an event.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.870
Now, events originate
asynchronously.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.450
And with events, a
producer has no expectation

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.640
of how the event might
be handled by a consumer.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.600
Let's take a look at a typical
event-driven architecture,

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.160
or an EDA.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.700
Multiple Event Producers
go ahead and produce events

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:33.400
and send them on to
Event Intermediaries.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.290
At the other end,
Event Consumers

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.300
subscribe to the
events in particular

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.670
that they're interested in
from specific producers.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.940
Now, EDAs solve
several of the problems

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.580
that we identified earlier.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.220
Let's start by looking
at scalability.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.120
Take a look at these two systems
that you see in front of you.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.970
One can handle 200
transactions a second,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:02.260
and the second can handle
100 transactions a second.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.990
Now, with just a
microservices architecture

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.790
where the communication
between them is synchronous,

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:11.080
a request response
style, the weakest link

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.450
dictates the peak
throughput of the system.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.380
But if you then graduate onto
event-driven microservices,

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.297
the weakest link now dictates
the sustained throughput

00:06:22.297 --> 00:06:22.880
of the system.

00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:27.290
Let's take a look
at extensibility

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.150
and how it's easier
to add services

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.550
with event-driven architectures.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.050
We have a couple
of Event Producers

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.070
and a couple of Event Consumers.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.410
We're going to go ahead and
add another Event Consumer.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.020
When we add an Event
Consumer, no changes

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:45.830
need to be made to any producer,
thus making extensibility

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:46.450
a lot easier.

00:06:49.128 --> 00:06:50.920
Let me highlight some
of the other benefits

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:54.250
of event-driven architectures.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.160
We can now decouple from
dependencies completely.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.910
The Event Intermediary
can handle

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:03.060
intermittent
unavailability of services.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.650
There's no more spider web.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.630
No more point-to-point
integrations.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.230
Event Producers can just fire
events and forget about them.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.240
Events are pushed, and this
replaces pull-based polling.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.710
And lastly, there's
an opportunity

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:21.070
to introduce a standardized
format, a uniform communication

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:22.990
format between services.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:24.700
For instance, Cloud
Events format.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:28.150
With an ideal event-driven
architecture system,

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.640
users no longer
have to be experts

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.440
in distributed computing.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.800
They can take event
ingestion, delivery, security,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:39.760
authorization, observability,
and error handling for granted

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.340
and just focus on their
application code for their use

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:43.348
case.

00:07:43.348 --> 00:07:45.890
This brings us to Google Cloud's
new offerings in this space.

00:07:48.655 --> 00:07:50.030
You're already
familiar with some

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:54.680
of Google Cloud's offerings to
help you with microservices.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.640
Cloud Run.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.340
Cloud Run helps you run any
stateless container on Google's

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.010
fully managed infrastructure.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.020
And there's Cloud
Run for Anthos,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.900
which helps you on any stateless
container on Google Kubernetes

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.080
engine, is Knative
conformant, and helps

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.770
you achieve portability to
other clouds or on prem.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.820
Today, we introduce to
you two new capabilities

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.750
to help you with
event-driven microservices,

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:27.740
Events for Cloud Run, and
Events for Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.080
Both these capabilities
help you easily build

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.600
event-driven architectures.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.850
Let's take a look at both
of these capabilities one

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.090
after the other.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.299
With Events for Cloud
Run, you can set up

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:42.679
triggers to act on events
sourced from all Google Cloud

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.860
sources via Cloud Audit Logs--

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:49.580
for instance, Cloud storage,
a popular Google Cloud Source,

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.630
via Cloud Audit Logs.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:56.020
You can also act on events
from custom applications that

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:59.540
publish events to Cloud PubSub.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.850
Events for Cloud Run
delivers these events

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.917
to Cloud Run targets.

00:09:04.917 --> 00:09:07.125
Let's now talk about Events
for Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:12.130
Events for Cloud run
for Anthos is Knative

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:15.370
based and incorporates the
concepts of Knative brokers

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.220
and triggers.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.990
With Events for
Cloud Run for Anthos,

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:23.310
you can act on events
sourced from all Google Cloud

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:28.160
sources via Cloud Audit
Logs, Cloud Storage and Cloud

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.210
Scheduler in particular,
which are native integrations,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.280
custom applications that
are publishing events

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.100
to Cloud Pub/Sub, and
also custom events.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.280
You can target Cloud Run for
Anthos targets, Kubernetes

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.210
services, and also any URL.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:49.100
With both Events for
Cloud Run and events

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.000
for Cloud Run for Anthos, they
both handle event ingestion,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.810
delivery, security,
authorization, observability,

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:59.720
and error handling for you.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.740
They both have the same delivery
guarantees as Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.450
At least once
delivery, for example.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.310
They both provide on demand
scalability and cost.

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:16.240
And you can operate both of
them through a UI, CLI, or API

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:16.900
interface.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.070
Both Events for Cloud Run and
Events for Cloud Run for Anthos

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:26.700
deliver events in
CloudEvents format.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.310
This also allows
you, the user, to use

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:32.940
CloudEvents available
SDKs in multiple languages

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:37.060
to be able to work
with your CloudEvents.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.800
We also provide libraries to
help you unmarshal the event

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.550
data within CloudEvents.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.610
I'd now like to take you through
a Sentiment Analysis demo,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.000
showing you the use of
Events for Cloud Run

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.510
and Events for Cloud
Run for Anthos.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.870
We're going to take
a complex image

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.990
and process it with an event
driven architecture system

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:00.850
that we're going to build.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:04.542
For example, take a look at
this image here of a family.

00:11:04.542 --> 00:11:07.000
Now, the holidays bring out
all kinds of different emotions

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.470
in different members
of the family.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:14.180
What we're going to try to do
here is from an input image,

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.700
draw out each family
member and determine what

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.900
their sentiments are,
what their sentiment score

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.650
is, all using event-driven
architectures.

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.370
Now, to accomplish such an
event driven architecture,

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:30.160
there's a few microservices
that we're going to need.

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:40.980
A Cloud Storage Bucket,
a Firestore Database,

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.755
and a few different
compute services.

00:11:43.755 --> 00:11:46.470
Now, these can be Cloud
Run services or Cloud Run

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.270
for Anthos services.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.190
To start, we need
one service which

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:53.430
can help us extract the
bounding boxes of faces

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:54.870
from the original image.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:00.680
We need another that
can extract face images

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:01.820
from those bounding boxes.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:08.240
A third that can run those faces
through an open neural network

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.730
exchange model, and
then generate an emotion

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:15.710
score from them.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:21.940
A fourth that can write those
scores to a Firestore database.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:25.540
And a fifth to render
a user interface.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.040
Now, let's take a
look at how we can

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.720
stitch these
services together in

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:30.428
an event-driven architecture.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.500
Let's go back to
this diagram, which

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:36.840
you're familiar with from
earlier in the presentation.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.570
We have Event Producers
and Event Consumers,

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.310
with an Event Intermediary
in the middle.

00:12:44.387 --> 00:12:46.220
Let's now bring in some
of the microservices

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:47.840
we need for our specific demo.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:54.360
Here, you see the Cloud Storage
Bucket, the Firestore Database,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.260
and the five compute
services that we identified.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.600
As a reminder, these
can be Cloud Run,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:05.302
Cloud Run for Anthos, or
a combination of the two.

00:13:05.302 --> 00:13:07.010
As we start to put
this service together,

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:11.980
let's walk through the flow and
start to stitch it together.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:16.090
A user uploads an image
to a Cloud Storage Bucket.

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.420
This should then fire an event
to an Event Intermediary.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.290
That event is then sent to
the first compute service.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:27.190
The first compute service
then sends a custom event

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:31.280
published to Pub/Sub back
to the Event Intermediary.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.830
This, then, reaches
the second service.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:36.940
The second service
extracts face images

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:38.440
and places them
in a Cloud Storage

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:42.370
Bucket, which then
fires another event back

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:44.090
to the Event Intermediary.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.120
This then moves on
to the third service,

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.240
which runs those faces through
the open neural network

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:52.660
exchange model and generates
the emotion scores.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.760
An event is then sent to
the Event Intermediary

00:13:54.760 --> 00:14:00.360
once again, which then reaches
the fourth compute service.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.910
The fourth compute
service writes a score

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.040
to the Firestore
Database, which then

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:08.040
fires another event to
the Event Intermediary

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.780
and updates the user interface.

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:13.590
I'm now going to show you a
demo in which we've already

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:16.920
put together several of the
components of the architecture

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.290
I described, but it's
missing one trigger.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.540
So we're going to
wire up that trigger,

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.930
and then we're going to also
show you the entire system

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:26.028
in operation.

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:31.600
We're now going to head to
the user interface of one

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:33.860
of our Cloud Run services.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:36.180
This is the second
Cloud Run service,

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.670
and we need to add
a trigger to it

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:39.920
to allow it to receive events
from the first Cloud Run

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.370
service.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.650
To do this, we're going to
head to the trigger step.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.460
We're going to start by
clicking on Add Trigger.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:53.070
We're asked to specify
a trigger name.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:57.230
We're going to leave the
default suggested value as is.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.450
We'll head to Event
Type, where we're

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:02.730
going to pick Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:05.435
This allows us to receive events
published as specific Cloud

00:15:05.435 --> 00:15:06.060
Pub/Sub topics.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.020
We're asked to select
a Cloud Pub/Sub topic,

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:13.562
and I'm going to select one that
I'd already created earlier.

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:20.260
As I click on Save, this will
complete trigger creation

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:23.156
and complete the setup of our
event driven architecture.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:30.050
